# InWove: every setting comes from the environment.
# On the server: copy this file to .env in the repository folder, fill it in, and run `docker compose up -d --build`
# (docs/hosting.md). The compose file reads this file twice: for its own substitutions (PUBLIC_HOST) and to hand every
# line to the room. For a run on your own machine without Docker, `npm run dev` reads it too.
# .env holds secrets (WORKSPACE_KEY, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, LLM_API_KEY, GITHUB_TOKEN). It is never committed (.gitignore
# lists it) and never copied to a shared place: only the server and the owner's password manager hold it.

# --- the server (docker-compose.yml)
# The room's public name, with no https:// and no path, for example inwove.example. Required by the compose file. It does
# two things: Caddy fetches an https certificate for it (its DNS record must already point at this server), and the
# compose file sets the room's PUBLIC_URL to https://<this>, so leave PUBLIC_URL below empty when you use the compose file.
PUBLIC_HOST=
# Not a setting for this file: docker-compose.yml sets NODE_ENV, PORT, DATA_DIR, DATABASE_URL (empty: embedded storage in
# the volume) and TRUSTED_CLIENT_IP_HEADER (the header Caddy writes the caller's address in) itself, and what it sets wins
# over anything written here. TRUSTED_CLIENT_IP_HEADER is safe only while the room's port is not published; see the comment
# there before changing anything about ports or what sits in front of Caddy.

# --- the workspace
# The workspace's own key (64 hex). It signs the weaver's posts and witnesses every log entry.
# Make one with: openssl rand -hex 32 (or npm run keygen). Required when NODE_ENV=production; keep it forever (the log
# is tied to it). Without it, a development key is made in DATA_DIR.
WORKSPACE_KEY=
WORKSPACE_NAME=InWove
# The address people reach it at (used in links and pull request text), e.g. https://interop.example. With the compose
# file, leave it empty: the compose file sets it from PUBLIC_HOST.
# It is also the room's Mastodon account, @room@<this host>: followers keep the address they
# followed, so announce the account only once this address is fixed (not a temporary tunnel).
PUBLIC_URL=
# The public Ed25519 multikey (z6Mk...) that may change the room's DID (not secret; the private half stays with the room's
# people, never on this server). Empty: the host keeps a fresh key in the data volume (update-key.txt) until this is set.
ROOM_DID_UPDATE_KEY=
# 1 turns on the room's Mastodon (ActivityPub) account. Off when empty: the room's record is for its members.
ACTIVITYPUB=
# The compose file sets PORT and NODE_ENV (production) itself. Here they are for a run on your own machine.
PORT=3000
NODE_ENV=development

# --- storage
# A Postgres connection string. Leave empty to run the same Postgres embedded (PGlite) in DATA_DIR, which is what the
# compose file does (it sets this itself; see above). The data must sit on encrypted storage either way.
DATABASE_URL=
# Where the room keeps its record and its other files. The compose file sets it to /data, the volume.
DATA_DIR=./data
# The projects shelf keeps a copy of the public code of the projects here (for agents to read and search) in
# DATA_DIR/shelf. This is how many MB of the data disk it may use; past it, the copies read longest ago are dropped
# and fetched again when asked for. It never uses GITHUB_TOKEN: public code only.
SHELF_MAX_MB=300

# --- the DTG board: the first source of the map (any other source can join it)
BOARD_REPO=Project-Weave-Tech/dtg-interop
BOARD_DATA_URL=https://project-weave-tech.github.io/dtg-interop/data.json
BOARD_REFRESH_MINUTES=15

# --- the weaver's model, option 1: Claude, through Anthropic's official SDK
# Used whenever ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is set. Model: Claude Opus 5.5 unless ANTHROPIC_MODEL says otherwise.
# A declined round is retried on another Claude model in the same call (server-side fallback).
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5
# low | medium | high | xhigh | max: how much the model thinks per round
LLM_EFFORT=medium
# Force a choice (anthropic | openai) instead of deciding by which key is set:
LLM_PROVIDER=

# --- the weaver's model, option 2: any OpenAI-compatible endpoint (used when no ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is set)
# Default: the Inference Cooperative gateway. Its enclave (TEE) models have ids starting
# with tinfoil/; with LLM_MODEL empty the weaver picks one of those first.
LLM_BASE_URL=https://gateway.inference.coop/v1
LLM_API_KEY=
LLM_MODEL=
LLM_PREFER_PREFIX=tinfoil/
LLM_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=120
# Without LLM_API_KEY the weaver still runs, as a plain tally, and every round says so.

# --- the loop
# the weaver speaks after a pause in the conversation, at most this often
WEAVER_INTERVAL_MINUTES=10
WEAVER_QUIET_MINUTES=2
# smart (the right Claude model per round) or a model id
WEAVER_MODEL=smart
# answers agents may give while their people are away, across the whole room, per hour
AWAY_ANSWERS_PER_HOUR=120
# entries a person (with their agents) may add per minute. The Trust Task door (/trust-tasks) follows it:
# twice this many documents a minute from one signer, eight times from one address.
RATE_PER_MINUTE=30
# The room's rules default to the agreed ones in rules.json; set these only to run your room differently
# (the Rules tab then shows the agreed value beside yours).
VETO_WINDOW_MINUTES=10
APPROVALS_REQUIRED=1
MAX_SKEW_MINUTES=10

# --- acting outside the workspace
# A GitHub token for a bot account. It needs: public_repo (fork dtg-interop, open pull requests
# and issues there) and, on WORKSPACE_REPO, contents + pull requests + commit statuses write.
# Without it, approved actions are prepared as links a person opens, and the log says so.
GITHUB_TOKEN=
WORKSPACE_REPO=Project-Weave-Tech/we-play-interop

# Linking GitHub: an agent joins only for repositories its person linked, and linking uses a GitHub OAuth App you make
# (docs/hosting.md, "Linking GitHub": callback address https://<your host>/api/github/callback). No scope (none is needed), PKCE; the room
# throws away the token GitHub hands it. Leave both empty and linking is off: the page says so and no agent can be added.
GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The people who host this room, by DID (comma separated): they mark an old account replaced once people who know
# its person have vouched for their new key (a lost key). Without it, that last step waits and the page says so.
ROOM_HOSTS=

# Signing in with a wallet (the You tab's second code): the host of the link the code carries. Empty: link.trustoverip.org,
# the host OpenVTC's wallets open. A public DNS name, never this room's own domain or under it: a link to the page's
# own domain opens in the browser, not the wallet. Sign-in itself needs no setting: it is on when the room has its
# Trust Task door, and /api/info (signIn) says what a wallet would still find missing in the room's DID document.
SIGNIN_LINK_HOST=

# The community whose membership pass this room takes: its did:webvh (for example an OpenVTC trust community's DID).
# The room reads where the community lives from that DID's own document, shows people where to join it
# (GET /api/community), and answers whether a pass someone shows is a current member's (POST /api/community/pass).
# It issues nothing and keeps nothing. Empty: no community, and the page and /api/info say so.
# Anything that is not a did:webvh is not used, and /api/info says that too.
COMMUNITY_DID=
# The https:// address the page's "Join" button opens. Empty: the community's own sign-in portal from its DID document,
# else /members/?join on its API address (docs/community-join-page.md).
COMMUNITY_JOIN_URL=

# --- the agent bridge (agent/bridge.js): these belong in the BRIDGE's environment, on its person's own machine.
# Never set them on the room's server: the server must not hold a person's credential.
# Keep the agent's notebook in a sealed room the person holds (all three, or none; see the README):
# the one line the person's trust agent hands an app (or VTA_CREDENTIAL_FILE: a file only their account can read)
# VTA_CREDENTIAL=
# the sealed room's DID, and the DID of the host it is registered at
# NOTEBOOK_ROOM=
# NOTEBOOK_HOST_DID=
# optional: the trust agent's address when the credential carries none; the host's door when its DID lists none;
# a few letters that say nothing, when the room also holds other things
# VTA_URL=
# NOTEBOOK_HOST_URL=
# NOTEBOOK_PREFIX=

# --- joining the community and its encrypted room, from the bridge (wpi-agent join-community, wpi-agent community-room).
# Also BRIDGE settings, with COMMUNITY_DID and VTA_CREDENTIAL above. The trust agent's address must be https://
# (plain http:// only for this machine, localhost). The credential must be registered as the person's own client with
# write scope: it can then sign as the person, within the trust agent's own policy and quota; a delegate is refused.
# The member DID that applies and speaks; your trust agent must hold a key for it.
# COMMUNITY_PERSONA=
# optional: the community's door, when you want to name it (https://, or http:// on this machine)
# COMMUNITY_URL=
# optional: which community room, when several are joined
# COMMUNITY_ROOM=
# yes: the agent may speak in the community's room on its own. Default (empty): it keeps a draft and you send it.
# COMMUNITY_TALK_AGENT_MAY_SAY=
